---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';

const modules = await getCollection('course');
const questions = await getCollection('questions');

const notationCount = modules.reduce((sum, m) => sum + m.data.notation.length, 0);
const exerciseCardCount = questions.filter((q) => q.data.reviewCardIds && q.data.reviewCardIds.length > 0).length;
const totalCards = notationCount + exerciseCardCount;
---
<BaseLayout title="Anki Export">
  <nav class="no-print breadcrumb"><a href="/">Start</a> / <a href="/curriculum">Curriculum</a> / Anki export</nav>
  <h1>Anki export</h1>
  <p>
    A downloadable snapshot, not a live sync: generated fresh from the site's own content every time you download
    it, but nothing here talks to Anki automatically and there's no account or server involved.
  </p>

  <h2>What's in it</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>{totalCards} cards</strong> total:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>{notationCount} notation cards</strong> — every module's symbol → meaning pairs (front: symbol, back: meaning + module ID), covering all {modules.length} modules. This is the bulk of the deck and needs no curation: it's pulled directly from each module's own notation box.</li>
    <li><strong>{exerciseCardCount} exercise recall cards</strong> — front: the exercise prompt, back: the full worked solution, for exercises specifically flagged as recall-worthy (via each exercise's <code>reviewCardIds</code> field). This is a smaller, curated subset, not every exercise: most exercises' full derivations are too long to make a good flashcard back, so only ones explicitly marked get a card.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Deliberately <strong>not</strong> included: a card per Retrieval Question. Those are meant to be self-tested
    from memory with no answer supplied in the source content, so auto-generating a "back" for them would mean
    either leaving it blank (not useful as a real flashcard) or fabricating an answer that was never actually
    written and verified — neither is worth doing. Use the module page itself for retrieval-question practice.
  </p>

  <h2>Download</h2>
  <p>
    <a href="/anki-export.tsv" download><strong>⬇ Download Anki TSV</strong></a>
  </p>

  <h2>Importing into Anki</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Download the file above.</li>
    <li>In Anki: <strong>File → Import</strong>, select the downloaded <code>.tsv</code> file.</li>
    <li>Anki should auto-detect tab as the field separator and 3 fields (Front, Back, Tags); if not, set those manually in the import dialog.</li>
    <li>Map the "Tags" column to Anki's Tags field so cards are organized by block/module (e.g. <code>notation::MATH</code>, <code>exercise::OPT-03</code>) — useful for filtering study sessions to one block at a time.</li>
    <li>Math renders via Anki's own LaTeX support (already wrapped in <code>[$]...[/$]</code>/<code>[$$]...[/$$]</code> tags in the export) — no extra setup needed on a standard Anki install.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Keeping it current</h2>
  <p>
    The file at <code>/anki-export.tsv</code> is regenerated at build time from whatever content currently
    exists, so as more modules/exercises are added, re-downloading later will pick them up automatically —
    re-import into Anki whenever you want an updated deck (Anki will add new cards and leave your existing
    review history on already-imported ones untouched, as long as note content hasn't changed).
  </p>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .breadcrumb { font-size: 0.85rem; opacity: 0.7; margin-bottom: 1rem; }
  .breadcrumb a { color: inherit; }
</style>
